<Page
  title="Editorial Policy | AnyAirDrop.com"
  description="How AnyAirDrop.com researches, writes, sources and corrects its guides on Wi-Fi, routers, phone hotspots and local file transfer."
  heading="Editorial policy"
  lead="The guides on AnyAirDrop.com explain how Wi-Fi and local file transfer work. This page says how they are written, what you can rely on, and how mistakes get fixed."
  updated={LEGAL_UPDATED}
>
  <h2>What we publish</h2>
  <p>
    Our <a href="/blog/">blog</a> explains networking technology that sits behind sending a file from one device to another:
    Wi-Fi, routers and modems, phone hotspots, Bluetooth, NFC and the protocols that let devices find each other. Each guide
    aims to answer a real question and to explain why things work the way they do, not only which button to press.
  </p>

  <h2>How a guide is researched</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Primary sources first.</strong> We draw on standards and specifications (IEEE, IETF RFCs, W3C, the Bluetooth
      SIG, the Wi-Fi Alliance, the NFC Forum) and on documentation from the companies that build the platforms, such as
      Apple, Google and Microsoft.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Every guide lists its sources</strong> at the bottom, so you can check any claim yourself.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Numbers are labeled.</strong> Figures such as maximum data rates are theoretical values derived from a
      standard, and we say so. Real-world results depend on your devices, walls, neighbors and network, so we explain what
      they depend on rather than promising a number.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No invented testing.</strong> Unless a guide says it contains our own measurements and describes how they were
      taken, it does not claim any. We do not present guesses, screenshots or results we did not produce as our own work.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How guides are written</h2>
  <p>
    Some guides are drafted with the help of AI writing tools, working from the sources listed in each guide.
    {FOUNDER.name}, who publishes AnyAirDrop.com, is responsible for everything that appears on the site. Menu names and
    settings on phones and computers change between versions and manufacturers, so where exact wording is not certain, a
    guide tells you what to look for instead of quoting a label that may be wrong.
  </p>

  <h2>Our own tool</h2>
  <p>
    AnyAirDrop.com also runs a free file-transfer tool. Where a guide mentions it, the guide says it is our own tool, and the
    explanation of how the technology works stands on its own without it. We do not write comparisons that rank products as
    "best", and we are not paid by any device maker, carrier or router brand.
  </p>

  <h2>Advertising and independence</h2>
  {
    ADS_ENABLED ? (
      <p>
        Guides show ads from Google AdSense. Ads are kept visually separate from the article, never appear on the transfer
        tool, and never change what a guide says. Nobody can pay to have a guide written, altered or removed.
      </p>
    ) : (
      <p>
        If advertising ever appears on this site, it will be clearly separate from the articles and will never change what a
        guide says. Nobody can pay to have a guide written, altered or removed.
      </p>
    )
  }

  <h2>Keeping guides up to date</h2>
  <p>
    Standards and phone software change. Each guide shows the date it was published and, when we change it in a meaningful
    way, the date it was updated. We recheck a guide when a standard it describes changes or when a reader reports a mistake.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    If you find something wrong, unclear or out of date, email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}?subject=Correction`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>
    with the page address and what you found. Factual errors are fixed as soon as they are confirmed, and the guide's updated
    date changes with them.
  </p>

  <h2>Trademarks</h2>
  <p>
    Names such as AirDrop, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, Android and iPhone belong to their owners. We use them only to refer to those
    products and technologies. AnyAirDrop.com is an independent service and is not affiliated with or endorsed by them.
  </p>
</Page>
